    "category": "Best Practices",
    "readTime": "2 min read",
    "content": "Real estate work involves many handoffs: an inquiry arrives, information is confirmed, a professional responds, documents are collected, and outstanding items are tracked. Software can help keep those handoffs visible without taking over the judgment and relationships at the center of the work.\n\nFor an Orlando real estate team, the starting point is a bounded process with a clear source of truth and a responsible person.\n\n## Make inquiry handling consistent\n\nAn assistant might organize an incoming message, identify the property involved, and prepare a response using approved information. It can also flag missing details and record the next step for the team.\n\nConfirm that listing information is current before using it. Do not invent property attributes, availability, financing terms, or expected returns. Route unfamiliar questions to an authorized professional.\n\n## Keep communications appropriate\n\nSet clear rules for when the system drafts, sends, or escalates a message. Respect the recipient's communication preferences and use the team's approved outreach policies.\n\nHave qualified professionals review the workflow for relevant obligations, including how it avoids inappropriate screening, steering, or discriminatory treatment. Sensitive decisions about housing eligibility should not be delegated to an unreviewed model.\n\n## Support document coordination\n\nA workflow can compare a checklist with received documents, identify outstanding items, and prepare an internal status summary. It should cite the relevant records so a coordinator can verify the result.\n\nDates, names, financial details, and contractual obligations require careful validation. Keep legal interpretation, commitments, and approvals with the appropriately qualified people.\n\n## An illustrative team workflow\n\nA new inquiry is linked to an existing record or flagged for review if the match is uncertain. The application gathers authorized information and prepares a draft response. A team member verifies it, sends it through the approved channel, and records the agreed next step.\n\nThis describes a possible design, not a client success story. The appropriate degree of automation depends on the actual process and review requirements.\n\n## Preserve state and avoid duplicate actions\n\nUse a durable record of who owns each task and whether it is pending, completed, or cancelled. Check for an existing action before retrying after a timeout.\n\nPeople may update the same transaction while an application is running. Resolve conflicting changes rather than overwriting newer information.\n\n## Measure the service experience\n\nTrack unresolved inquiries, correction effort, missed handoffs, and time spent preparing routine work. Do not assume that faster replies automatically create more closed transactions.\n\nExplore [agentic applications](/services/agentic-applications) and [workflow automation](/services/workflow-automation) that help professionals stay informed and in control."
