  {
    "slug": "ai-implementation-mistakes-central-florida",
    "title": "10 AI Implementation Mistakes to Avoid",
    "description": "Common planning and engineering pitfalls, from unclear success criteria and weak data to overbroad permissions, missing fallbacks, and unsupported ROI claims.",
    "date": "2026-01-31",
    "author": "Vektar team",
    "image": "/blog-fallback.svg",
    "category": "Strategy",
    "readTime": "3 min read",
    "content": "AI projects can fail for ordinary software reasons as well as model-specific ones. Unclear ownership, poor data, and an unfinished integration can undermine a good demonstration.\n\nThis checklist is an educational guide for Central Florida teams planning an implementation. It is not a claim about a particular number of client projects or a measured industry failure rate.\n\n## 1. Choosing a tool before a problem\n\nStart with the user's task and the current bottleneck. Compare AI with a conventional integration, better search, or a simpler process change. The model should serve the application, not define its purpose.\n\n## 2. Skipping the baseline\n\nMeasure the existing process before changing it. Agree on how you will count completed work, correction effort, delays, and cost. Otherwise, an improvement can be difficult to distinguish from a change in workload.\n\n## 3. Ignoring the source data\n\nCheck missing values, stale documents, inconsistent identifiers, and access boundaries. A plausible answer based on the wrong record is still wrong. Assign responsibility for keeping source information current.\n\n## 4. Giving the application too much authority\n\nSeparate read access, draft preparation, and external actions. Enforce permissions in application code and require approval where the consequences warrant it. Do not let instructions inside retrieved content expand the system's authority.\n\n## 5. Testing only clean examples\n\nInclude ambiguity, contradictions, unusual requests, and adversarial input. Test the full workflow when tools fail or people change a record mid-task. Save meaningful failures as regression cases.\n\n## 6. Treating an AI reviewer as a guarantee\n\nA second model may help identify problems, but it can share the first model's errors. Preserve sources, use deterministic validation where possible, and keep accountable human review for consequential decisions.\n\n## 7. Forgetting retries and recovery\n\nA timeout can leave an action's outcome uncertain. Reconcile the destination before retrying a write, protect against duplicates, and provide cancellation. Make a manual fallback part of the design.\n\n## 8. Counting capacity as guaranteed revenue\n\nTime saved can be useful without immediately creating cash savings or sales. Include review, training, integration maintenance, and software usage. Label projections as estimates and state their assumptions.\n\n## 9. Leaving ownership until launch\n\nDecide who reviews failures, updates data, manages permissions, and handles incidents before the system goes live. Provide documentation that the operating team can use without the original builder present.\n\n## 10. Expanding before the evidence supports it\n\nStart with a bounded release and compare it with the baseline. Wider permissions, more users, and additional integrations each change the risk. Expand deliberately and keep a rollback path.\n\n## Turn the checklist into a release gate\n\nFor each item, record an owner and the evidence needed to close it. Review unresolved issues before launch rather than hiding them in a general confidence statement.\n\nExplore [Vektar's services](/services) for the relationship between useful application design, reliable integrations, and human oversight."
  }
